---
// LICENSING — full rewrite, 2026-09-07.
//
// WHAT WAS WRONG. The page read as commentary about a licensing philosophy rather than as a
// licensing page. It had no comparison of what is published against what is licensed, which is
// the single artefact a buyer comes to a page like this for; it explained the four layers before
// establishing the model; and it mixed pre-booking mechanics into the middle of the licence terms.
//
// THE COMMERCIAL POSITION, decided and stated here so it does not drift:
//
//   THE PUBLISHED RELEASE IS NOT MIT AND IS NOT OSI OPEN SOURCE. It is a research licence:
//   unrestricted for research, teaching and independent evaluation, and licensed for anything in
//   production. Earlier copy across the site said "MIT, permanently", carried over from
//   Commercial Plan `18 §5`. That is superseded by a founder decision and corrected everywhere.
//
//   This is the stronger position, not a weaker one, and the page says so: the mechanism is fully
//   readable so it can be checked, and there is no ambiguity about who pays. Credibility in these
//   markets rests on 383 reproducible experiments that anyone can run.
//
//   What is licensed is the production system and, more precisely, the accountability: the
//   submission-grade artefact layer, the bound calibrated and validated for one deployment,
//   reproducible builds, support, indemnity, and a counterparty who signs. `18 §3` carries the
//   sentence the page turns on: open-source software has no counterparty. An institution cannot
//   put a licence in a filing, and cannot sue it.
//
// STRUCTURE follows how these pages are actually built (HashiCorp's licensing FAQ, the open-core
// edition-comparison convention): model first, then a comparison table, then the layers, then
// terms, then a grouped FAQ. Not a philosophy essay.
//
// NO RELEASE-VERSION LANGUAGE anywhere on this page. The site does not discuss internal versions.
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import PageBanner from '../components/PageBanner.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../components/Eyebrow.astro';
import Accordion from '../components/Accordion.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import GetInTouch from '../components/GetInTouch.astro';

// The comparison a buyer actually comes here for. `published` is the research release; `licensed` is
// what the deployment licence adds. The rows are ordered so the free column runs out first — the
// point of the table is where it stops.
const matrix = [
	{ item: 'The training architecture itself', published: true, note: 'The mechanism, in full. Run it, modify it, ship what is built with it.' },
	{ item: 'The telemetry channels', published: true, note: 'All five layers, emitted natively, exactly as the paper describes them.' },
	{ item: 'The correction mechanism and its bounds', published: true, note: 'Eight diagnoses, their triggers, and the per-diagnosis ceilings.' },
	{ item: 'The paper and every experiment behind it', published: true, note: '383 controlled runs, each regenerating from seed, with the reproducibility checklist.' },
	{ item: 'Teaching, publishing and independent evaluation', published: true, note: 'Use it in a course, cite it in a paper, or evaluate our claims against it. No permission needed, and no notification to us.' },

	{ item: 'Submission-grade record generation', published: false, note: 'The record in the shape a reviewer expects to receive, rather than a research log.' },
	{ item: 'The diff engine', published: false, note: 'Two records compared line by line. The only question a reviewer actually asks.' },
	{ item: 'Cryptographic signing and a frozen reader', published: false, note: 'A hash over a canonical body, a detached signature, and a reader binary the licensee keeps.' },
	{ item: 'The bound, calibrated for the domain', published: false, note: 'Derived against the data and the institution\'s risk profile, then validated. Not a default.' },
	{ item: 'Integration into the customer stack', published: false, note: 'Bindings for the training infrastructure, the federation, the imaging pipeline.' },
	{ item: 'Version-pinned reproducible builds', published: false, note: 'Hash-pinned, with the means to verify that the running binary is the one validated.' },
	{ item: 'Support against an agreed response time', published: false, note: 'With the person who built the architecture, not a tier-one queue.' },
	{ item: 'Indemnity', published: false, note: 'Contractual liability, which is the thing an open licence structurally cannot offer.' },
	{ item: 'A counterparty who signs the bound', published: false, note: 'Somebody who will stand behind the declaration under questioning.' },
];

const layers = [
	{
		tag: 'RESEARCH LICENCE',
		name: 'The research release',
		terms: 'Free for research, teaching and evaluation. Permanent.',
		body: 'The architecture, the telemetry, the correction mechanism and every experiment behind them, released in full on publication. The manuscript is under peer review and the release follows acceptance, a commitment written into the licence rather than an intention. From that day a researcher, a student, a model-risk validator or a regulator can run it, reproduce our results, teach from it and publish about it without asking us and without paying us. Evaluation access is available before then under agreement. Production use sits under the deployment licence.',
		revenue: 'Purpose: the mechanism can be checked.',
	},
	{
		tag: 'SOURCE-AVAILABLE',
		name: 'The artefact layer',
		terms: 'Free for research. Licensed for regulated commercial deployment.',
		body: 'The record generator, the diff engine, the signing and versioning machinery, and the templates mapped onto the frameworks these markets file under. Readable by anyone, because a format nobody can inspect is a format nobody adopts. Converts to a permissive licence on a date fixed in the licence text.',
		revenue: 'Purpose: the format, not the fee.',
	},
	{
		tag: 'COMMERCIAL',
		name: 'The deployment licence',
		terms: 'Annual, per institution, on customer infrastructure.',
		body: 'Integration into the customer environment, the bound calibrated and validated for the domain, reproducible builds, support, and indemnity. This is the licence, and essentially all revenue is here.',
		revenue: 'Where the commercial relationship lives.',
	},
	{
		tag: 'ENGAGEMENT',
		name: 'Assurance work',
		terms: 'Priced per engagement.',
		body: 'Calibrating a bound for a domain nobody has calibrated before, drafting a change-control plan, authoring the validation report, and appearing to defend it. Low volume, high value, and the reason a deployment licence renews.',
		revenue: 'The layer that cannot be copied at all.',
	},
];

const clauses = [
	{
		name: 'Artefact survival',
		what: 'On termination the licensee keeps a perpetual, irrevocable right to read, retain and reproduce every record generated during the term, together with a frozen reader binary.',
		why: 'Regulatory obligations outlive a commercial relationship. A customer who suspects they could lose their audit trail by not renewing will never sign in the first place, so this clause costs us nothing and makes the contract signable.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Bound immutability',
		what: 'The declared bound and modification set are fixed when the licence executes. They change only by a documented, mutually signed amendment that generates its own record.',
		why: 'If we can quietly alter the bound, the bound is worth nothing and every filing that references it is void. Our own product goes under change control, and we volunteer that rather than waiting to be asked.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Reproducible build attestation',
		what: 'The licence carries hash-pinned builds and the means to verify that the binary running in the customer environment is the exact artefact that was validated.',
		why: 'Technical documentation requirements and software provenance expectations both point here. It costs us a build pipeline and it removes an entire class of question from a security review.',
	},
];

const never = [
	{ rule: 'No per-seat pricing', why: 'The buyer is an institution carrying an obligation, not a team with users. Charging per seat would price the wrong thing and cap the deal at headcount.' },
	{ rule: 'No metering that phones home', why: 'In a hospital enclave or a bank network, outbound telemetry is a security review we would not pass, and should not.' },
	{ rule: 'No free tier touching regulated data', why: 'Unbounded support exposure and real liability, in exchange for a lead. The research release is free; a free production tier is not the same thing.' },
	{ rule: 'No relicensing of the research core', why: 'Publishing and then closing after adoption would destroy the credibility every commercial claim here rests on. The research release is permanent once made, and that is a commitment written into the licence rather than a default.' },
	{ rule: 'No delivery-date commitments', why: 'We state what is built, what is being built, and what is not started. We will not put a date on the third category.' },
];
---

<Page title="Licensing: one product, one licence" description={"A research licence, free for research and teaching. Production deployment is licensed, because an open licence has no counterparty to stand behind a filing."}>
	<PageBanner
		eyebrow="LICENSING"
		title="We publish the mechanism. We license the accountability."
		trail={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Licensing' }]}
	/>

	<section class="section container">
		<div class="lic-intro">
			<div>
				<p class="lic-lede">
					Everything that makes the architecture work is released on publication and stays
					released: the mechanism, the telemetry, the correction system, the paper and all 383
					experiments behind it. The manuscript is under peer review; the release follows
					acceptance and the terms are already fixed. From that day a researcher, a student, a
					model-risk validator or a regulator can read it, run it, reproduce every result and
					publish about it, permanently and without asking us. That commitment is why any of
					this is checkable rather than merely asserted.
				</p>
				<p class="lic-licence-link">
					<a href="/LICENCE.txt">Read the full licence text</a>. It is a working draft, published now
					so a university or an enterprise legal team can clear it before the release lands rather
					than after.
				</p>
				<p class="lic-lede">
					Running it in production is licensed. The distinction is deliberate and it is not
					hidden anywhere in a footnote: research and teaching are free, and an institution
					putting this into a regulated deployment takes a licence.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="lic-turn">
				<p>
					Published code has no counterparty. It cannot be named in a filing, it cannot stand behind
					a declaration, and there is nobody to hold to it.
				</p>
				<p class="lic-turn-sub">
					A licence supplies what the repository structurally cannot: a named party that
					calibrated the bound, validated it against the domain, and signed it. In markets that
					buy on auditability, that is what is actually being purchased.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- The artefact a buyer comes to a licensing page for, and the page did not have one. -->
	<section class="band band--surface section reveal">
		<div class="container">
			<Eyebrow>WHAT IS PUBLISHED, AND WHAT IS LICENSED</Eyebrow>
			<h2 class="section-title">The line, drawn once.</h2>
			<p class="section-lede">
				The rows are in order. The free column runs out partway down, and where it stops is the
				product.
			</p>

			<div class="lic-table">
				<div class="lic-table-head">
					<span></span>
					<span>Research licence</span>
					<span class="lic-col-lit">Deployment licence</span>
				</div>
				{matrix.map((r) => (
					<div class={`lic-row${r.published ? '' : ' lic-row--paid'}`}>
						<div class="lic-row-item">
							<p class="lic-row-name">{r.item}</p>
							<p class="lic-row-note">{r.note}</p>
						</div>
						<div class="lic-cell">
							<span class="lic-cell-label" aria-hidden="true">Research</span>
							<span class="lic-mark" aria-label={r.published ? 'Included in the research release' : 'Not in the research release'}>
								{r.published ? '●' : '—'}
							</span>
						</div>
						<div class="lic-cell">
							<span class="lic-cell-label" aria-hidden="true">Deployment</span>
							<span class="lic-mark lic-mark--lit" aria-label="Included in the deployment licence">●</span>
						</div>
					</div>
				))}
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow>THE FOUR LAYERS</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="section-title">One of them is the business, and it is not the one people expect.</h2>
		<ol class="lic-layers">
			{layers.map((l, i) => (
				<li class={`lic-layer${i === 2 ? ' lic-layer--revenue' : ''}`}>
					<div class="lic-layer-head">
						<span class="lic-layer-tag">{l.tag}</span>
						<h3>{l.name}</h3>
						<p class="lic-layer-terms">{l.terms}</p>
					</div>
					<div>
						<p class="lic-layer-body">{l.body}</p>
						<p class="lic-layer-revenue">{l.revenue}</p>
					</div>
				</li>
			))}
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section class="band band--dark section reveal">
		<div class="container">
			<Eyebrow>WHAT THE FIGURE IS BUILT FROM</Eyebrow>
			<h2 class="lic-dark-title">Priced against exposure, not against a feature list.</h2>
			<p class="lic-dark-lede">
				A rate card would price the software. What an institution is buying here is the removal of
				a specific, quantified risk, and that is worth a different amount to a consortium than to
				a bank. The figure is set per institution and written into the contract. Four things
				determine it.
			</p>
			<ol class="lic-price">
				<li><span class="tabular">01</span><div><p>What the problem currently costs, which is the only anchor that means anything.</p></div></li>
				<li><span class="tabular">02</span><div><p>Which applications are turned on, and how much of the model portfolio is in scope.</p></div></li>
				<li><span class="tabular">03</span><div><p>Whether the bound for the domain already exists or has to be calibrated and validated from nothing.</p></div></li>
				<li><span class="tabular">04</span><div><p>What we are asked to sign, and therefore what we are carrying.</p></div></li>
			</ol>
			<p class="lic-dark-note">
				Annual, per institution, on-premise. No per-seat component and no consumption meter.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow>THREE CLAUSES THAT ARE NOT STANDARD</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="section-title">Written to survive the questions a procurement team is paid to ask.</h2>
		<div class="lic-clauses">
			{clauses.map((c, i) => (
				<article class="lic-clause">
					<p class="lic-clause-n tabular">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</p>
					<h3>{c.name}</h3>
					<p class="lic-clause-what">{c.what}</p>
					<p class="lic-clause-why"><span>Why</span> {c.why}</p>
				</article>
			))}
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band band--surface section reveal">
		<div class="container">
			<Eyebrow>DEPLOYMENT</Eyebrow>
			<h2 class="section-title">It runs where the data already is, and there is no other option.</h2>
			<div class="lic-deploy">
				<p>
					Every commercial deployment is on-premise or inside their own cloud environment. There
					is no hosted tier and there will not be one. Model weights and training telemetry
					leaving a controlled network is a disqualifying finding in a hospital enclave, a bank,
					or a classified environment, and those are the markets this exists for.
				</p>
				<p>
					The practical consequence is that the licensee holds the artefact. Nothing checks in, nothing
					reports usage, and nothing stops working if we do. That is a constraint we designed
					around rather than a feature we chose, and it shapes every commercial term above.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow>WHAT WE WILL NOT DO</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="section-title">Five commitments, with the reasoning attached.</h2>
		<ul class="lic-never">
			{never.map((n) => (
				<li>
					<p class="lic-never-rule">{n.rule}</p>
					<p class="lic-never-why">{n.why}</p>
				</li>
			))}
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow>LICENSING QUESTIONS</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="section-title">The ones that decide whether this is workable.</h2>
		<div class="faq-group lic-faq">
			<Accordion id="lic-fork" question="Why publish the mechanism at all?">
				<p>
					Because a claim nobody can check is worth nothing to the people who buy this. A
					validator, an assurance lab or a regulator can take the published release, run all 383
					experiments, and confirm every number on this site independently. A closed
					reimplementation of a published method is worth less to that audience, not more, which
					is why publishing is a commercial decision rather than a generous one.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="lic-commercial-free" question="Can we use the published release in production?">
				<p>
					Research, teaching, benchmarking and independent evaluation are free and always will
					be. Production is licensed. A university group, a student or a validator checking our claims
					owes us nothing and needs no permission.
					Where it goes into something the institution operates, that is a licence, and the
					licence is where the calibrated bound, the reproducible builds, the support and the
					indemnity live.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="lic-relicense" question="Will the research terms be withdrawn later?">
				<p>
					No, and the licence text says so. Free research and teaching access is permanent.
					Publishing a method, waiting for academic adoption and then withdrawing it is a move
					that works exactly once, and it would take the credibility of every commercial claim
					we make with it. The published release is the reason the rest is believable, so it is
					not something we would trade.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="lic-when-public" question="When does the code become public?">
				<p>
					When the review period on the paper closes. Peer review sets that timing, not us, so
					there is no date on this page. The mechanism is available before then on request: the manuscript and the supplementary
					material go to serious evaluators who ask.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="lic-end" question="If we stop paying, do we lose our records?">
				<p>
					No, and the licence says so explicitly. A perpetual, irrevocable right to read, retain
					and reproduce every record generated during the term survives termination, together
					with a frozen reader binary held permanently. Regulatory obligations outlive commercial
					relationships, so access to the audit trail has to outlive them too.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="lic-audit" question="Does anything phone home for licence compliance?">
				<p>
					Nothing. No telemetry, no usage meter, no licence server, no call-out of any kind. In a
					hospital enclave, a bank network or a classified environment, anything that reaches
					outward fails the security review before anyone opens the commercial terms. The
					product is built for the environment it is sold into rather than for our convenience.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="lic-oss-policy" question="Our software policy requires review of every dependency. What does our counsel review?">
				<p>
					Two documents, and both arrive before engagement rather than after. The research
					licence, which permits reading, running, reproducing and teaching, and reserves
					production use. And the deployment licence, an ordinary commercial agreement carrying
					the three clauses set out above. Neither is a copyleft licence and neither imposes any
					obligation on code written alongside it.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="lic-indemnity" question="What exactly is indemnified?">
				<p>
					Scope is set with the customer’s counsel, because a consortium and a bank are not carrying the
					same exposure and a single template would serve neither well. What does not vary: the
					bound we declare is one we will defend under questioning, and we do not sign
					declarations we are not prepared to stand behind.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
		</div>

		<div class="lic-cta">
			<Button variant="primary" href="/contact">Discuss licence terms</Button>
			<Button variant="secondary" href="/product">See the product</Button>
		</div>
	</section>

	<GetInTouch variant="pre-book" heading="Licensing conversations start with what an institution has to be able to prove." />
</Page>
